<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/expert/computational-and-parametric-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational design is <strong>architecture's software turn</strong>: buildings described as
    rule systems (parameters and relationships), machines exploring option spaces, and
    fabrication data flowing straight from model to robot. The pencil didn't disappear — it
    gained a compiler. The accreditation criterion this lesson serves frames the field as
    <em>research and innovation</em>: engaging in architectural research, and testing and
    evaluating innovations in the field. That framing is exactly right, and it disciplines the
    subject: parametric tools are not a style but a scientific instrument — a way of stating
    design intentions precisely enough to test them — and the profession's credibility with
    them rests on the same loop every experimental field uses: hypothesis, model, measurement,
    revision. A building designed computationally and never evaluated is a hypothesis published
    without a results section.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="rules, lineage, optimisation, critiques" widget="equationStep" config={paramCfg} />

  <h2>Parametric modelling: geometry driven by rules</h2>
  <p>
    A conventional CAD model records <em>results</em> (this line is here); a parametric model
    records <em>logic</em> (this line is the offset of that surface by a function of the sun
    angle). The mechanism: a dependency graph of parameters and operations — curve, offset,
    panelise, sort by exposure — in visual programming environments like Grasshopper (built on
    Rhino, 2007) or scripting in Python. Change the parameter (the sun angle, the floor-to-floor
    height, the panel width) and the entire building updates coherently, because the model
    recomputes relationships rather than redrawing lines. The consequences are practical before
    they are aesthetic: <strong>design space becomes explorable</strong> (one model, thousands of
    variants), <strong>coordination becomes computational</strong> (the BIM model is a parametric
    database — change the atrium and every section, schedule and clash-check updates),
    <strong>geometry becomes buildable</strong> (each doubly-curved panel gets its own
    fabrication data automatically — the only way a 12,000-panel facade like the ones on
    Foster's or Hadid's buildings could ever be documented), and <strong>rules become
    explicit</strong> — the moments where the model misbehaves are the moments your
    understanding of the problem was vague. That last property is the quiet pedagogical power:
    parametric modelling is architecture forced to say what it means.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "parametric design means curvy buildings." The curves are a
    period style that happened to be built with these tools; the tools themselves are logic,
    equally at home optimising a repeated balcony section, spacing a hospital's handrail
    brackets to code, or testing a hundred plan options for a rectangular school. The style
    debate (below) and the method are separable, and confusing them costs the method its
    credibility.
  </p>

  <h2>The lineage: form-finding before the computer</h2>
  <p>
    The field's deepest tools predate the microprocessor, because their origin is <strong>form-finding</strong>
    — letting a physical system compute a shape. Gaudí's hanging-chain models for the Colònia
    Güell chapel (c. 1900) inverted gravity: a network of weighted strings finds the pure
    compression shape, which read right-side-up gives the vault geometry — the Sagrada Família
    (under construction since 1882) still grows from that method, now with parametric and
    stereo-photogrammetric descendants. Frei Otto's soap films (Munich's Olympic Stadium,
    1972) computed minimal surfaces — membranes spanning between boundaries at uniform
    tension — and his Institute for Lightweight Structures made "let the physics draw" a
    research programme; his 2015 Pritzker was awarded the day after his death, posthumously by
    hours, for exactly this. The algorithmic turn proper arrived when aerospace and shipbuilding
    software did: Gehry's office adopted CATIA (Dassault's aviation tool) for the Guggenheim
    Bilbao (1997) because no architectural package could document its titanium panels — the
    machine-tool logic of aerospace geometry, imported wholesale. The lineage's lesson: the
    computation is the latest link in a century-old chain whose constant is that <em>form is
    found by simulating forces</em> — chains, films, and now finite elements and gradient
    descent do the same job at higher resolution.
  </p>

  <h2>Optimisation and generative design: the computer proposes</h2>
  <p>
    Once a design is parametric, it can be <em>searched</em>. The mechanism: define constraints
    (site, code, budget) and objectives (daylight autonomy, usable area, embodied carbon, cost),
    let an algorithm — genetic algorithms, simulated annealing, topological optimisation —
    generate and score thousands of candidates, and return a <strong>Pareto front</strong>: the
    set of designs where no objective can improve without another degrading. The human's job
    moves from drawing one answer to choosing among defensible ones — which is more
    consequential, not less, because the choice of objectives <em>is</em> the design ethics.
    The canonical worked case is Autodesk's generative-design study for its own Toronto office:
    given head-count, circulation and light requirements, the system generated over 10,000
    viable floor-plate variants, and the team selected from the Pareto set rather than
    sketching from scratch. In structural practice, topology optimisation (remove material
    where stress does not flow) produces the organic-looking bracing now common in tall
    buildings — the shape computed by Newton's laws, not drawn. The honest limits matter:
    objectives are proxies (the alignment chapter's specification problem, architectural
    edition — an algorithm told to maximise daylight will happily remove every wall), and
    search finds what the model can express, never what the model cannot represent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the computer found the optimum, so the design is objective."
    The optimum is optimum <em>with respect to the chosen objective function</em>, and choosing
    it is a values decision made by a person. Two offices optimising the same site — one for
    daylight, one for rental area — will get different "optimal" buildings, both correct.
    Optimisation relocates the judgement; it does not remove it.
  </p>

  <h2>Engaging in architectural research</h2>
  <p>
    PC.5's first criterion — engaging and participating in architectural research — describes a
    set of methods, not an academic detour. The field's research modes: <strong>performance
    research</strong> (simulated and measured building physics: energy, daylight, acoustics,
    airflow — the sustainable-design chapter's toolkit), <strong>post-occupancy evaluation
    (POE)</strong> — systematically surveying and measuring buildings in use (satisfaction,
    comfort, energy, productivity) and feeding results back into the next design; the findings
    are consistently humbling, from the performance gap (in-use energy at 1.5–2.5× prediction)
    to the discovery that iconic features can underperform their modest competitors,
    <strong>design-as-research</strong> (the studio experiment: controlled variation across a
    design series, documented and shared — the visual-arts chapter's sustained-project method
    at building scale), and <strong>computational research</strong> (new tools, algorithms and
    workflows, published with the reproducibility norms of any field). The professional
    infrastructure exists: RIBA and the AIA run research agendas; journals (<em>Building
    Research and Information</em>, <em>Automation in Construction</em>) peer-review the work;
    and the strongest practices run internal research groups whose output is measured in
    facades built. The worked habit that defines research literacy: for any design claim —
    "biophilic design improves wellbeing", "open plan improves collaboration" — ask for the
    study, its sample and its controls; the literature answers some of these (green space:
    reasonably supported; open-plan collaboration: famously contradicted, with measured
    face-to-face interaction <em>falling</em> in some open-plan transitions) and the practice
    that cites evidence beats the practice that cites vibes.
  </p>

  <h2>Testing and evaluating innovations in the field</h2>
  <p>
    PC.5's second criterion — testing and evaluating innovations — is the experimental
    discipline that separates innovation from fashion. The mechanism is staged verification:
    <strong>prototypes and mockups</strong> (a facade panel full-scale in the weather before
    5,000 are fabricated; the curtain-wall industry's standard test sequence — air, water,
    structural — exists because facade failures are systemic and expensive),
    <strong>pilot installations</strong> (one floor, one wing, one building before the estate
    rolls out), <strong>instrumented monitoring</strong> (sensors measuring what the innovation
    promised: a "smart" facade's actual heat flux, a new ventilation strategy's measured CO₂
    and comfort), and <strong>structured evaluation against the original claim</strong> — the
    research loop closed. The exemplary case is the long arc of mass timber: laboratory fire
    testing (char rates, connection performance), monitored pilot buildings (moisture sensors
    through construction), measured acoustic and vibration performance, and published
    whole-life carbon accounting — a decade of staged evidence that carried CLT from novelty
    to code recognition in multiple jurisdictions. The negative case is just as instructive:
    double-skin facades, aggressively marketed in the 2000s, frequently underperformed their
    claims in measured post-occupancy studies — a technology whose evaluation arrived after
    its adoption, at the industry's expense. The rule the criterion encodes: <em>the field,
    not the brochure, is the laboratory</em>, and every innovation is a hypothesis the building
    will test regardless of whether you designed the test.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "BIM is just 3D CAD with more polygons." Building
  Information Modelling's real object is the <em>database</em>: every wall, duct and
  damper carries structured data — cost, fire rating, maintenance interval, carbon
  figure — that schedules, clash detection and whole-life analysis read directly. The
  model is a contract between disciplines, which is why BIM adoption is organisational
  change first and software second.
  </p>

  <p>
    The optimiser's own mechanics deserve a word, because they shape what gets built.
    Gradient-based methods need smooth, differentiable objectives; the design problems
    architecture actually poses are discrete, multi-objective and non-differentiable,
    which is why evolutionary and genetic algorithms dominate (they mutate parameter
    sets, keep the fittest, and never need a gradient). Their honest output is not one
    building but a <strong>Pareto front</strong> — the set of designs where no objective
    improves without another degrading — and the act of choosing a point on that front
    is exactly the judgement that remains irreducibly the designer's.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The critique that shaped the field.</strong> "Parametricism" — Patrik Schumacher's
    2008 manifesto claiming parametric logic as a unified avant-garde style — provoked the
    field's most useful argument: critics (from Mario Carpo's histories to sceptical
    practitioners) charged that signature curvature drove cost and embodied carbon upward while
    delivering unmeasured benefits. The resolution the mature profession reached: computation
    in service of <em>performance and constructability</em>, with the style question secondary —
    and the same scrutiny now aimed at AI-generated imagery, which can sketch anything and
    build nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Digital fabrication and the twin.</strong> The pipeline's downstream is
    manufacturing: CNC cutting, robotic winding and printing, 3-D-printed concrete — where a
    doubly-curved panel costs little more than a flat one, reversing an economics that shaped
    form for centuries (what is cheap is now what is <em>described</em>, not what is
    <em>rectangular</em>). Upstream of occupancy, the BIM model becomes a <strong>digital
    twin</strong>: a live, sensor-fed model used to operate the building — the control-systems
    chapter's feedback loop, running a campus — and, crucially, a research instrument, since
    the twin's predictions can be compared against the building's behaviour continuously. The
    twin is where the parametric model finally meets its judge.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>AI in the studio, and the open questions.</strong> Generative image models as
    concept engines, LLMs drafting specifications and code-compliance checks, and learned
    surrogates making whole-building energy and daylight evaluation near-instant — the
    workflow is absorbing the generative-models chapter's machinery. The open research
    questions are the ones every tool-bearing field faces: hallucinated code citations in
    compliance tools (verification still required); bias inherited from training corpora
    (whose buildings trained the model?); and authorship/provenance regimes for generated
    design. The through-line for this lesson's criterion holds: in a research-literate
    profession, every new tool arrives as a claim, gets a test protocol, and earns its place
    in the workflow the way mass timber did — measured, published, revisable.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Terzidis, Algorithmic Architecture; the Gaudi and Frei Otto form-finding literature; Iveson on generative design in practice (Autodesk research).</p>
</TopicPage>
